#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""USGS 3DHP polygon water for the thirteen rollout stations,
baked at build time for build25/patch_3dhp_all.py (the Dallas
gate build's data - build25/dallas3dhp_data.py - stays separate
and owns Dallas).

Source: USGS 3D Hydrography Program (3DHP), live service 3DHP_all
on hydro.nationalmap.gov, layer 60 (Waterbody), pulled 2026-11-01:
every waterbody of at least 0.015 km2 intersecting the station's
legacy bake box (the rendered overview block + 2 km), clipped to
that box, unioned.

The arrays here are FULLY BAKED, like the original ht-polywater
module was: the bridge bands and road strips are already cut out
(GEOS differences at build time; see patch_3dhp_all.py's
docstring for the method and for why the cutting is baked rather
than re-cut in the deploy chain). Rings are simplified to 1 m,
stored at 6 decimal places, closed, with pieces under 20 m2
dropped.

Provenance, recorded honestly per station: PROVENANCE_3DHP gives
the 3DHP workunit source for each station's content. "EDH" would
mean the station's area is covered by an elevation-derived
hydrography workunit (3DHP_all layer 92); at pull time
(2026-11-01) NO rollout station was covered by an EDH workunit -
every pulled waterbody carries an NHD workunit id, so all
thirteen are recorded as NHD-sourced content served through
3DHP, the same finding as the Dallas gate build. 3DHP is the
live, maintained USGS product either way.
"""

